```python
LOG = 20  # enough for n up to ~2^20
up = [[0] * n for _ in range(LOG)]  # up[k][node] = 2^k-th ancestor of node

def build(n, parent):
    for node in range(n):
        up[0][node] = parent[node]  # base case: 2^0 = direct parent
    for k in range(1, LOG):
        for node in range(n):
            up[k][node] = up[k - 1][up[k - 1][node]]  # jump 2^(k-1), then 2^(k-1) again
```

Each column is built entirely from the **previous** column — `up[k][node]` is "the 2^(k-1)-th ancestor of the 2^(k-1)-th ancestor," which composes to exactly 2^k hops. This is a small dynamic-programming recurrence, not a from-scratch computation per cell, which is what keeps the whole table O(n log n) to build rather than O(n²).

