# BUG: skips the depth-equalization phase entirely
def lca_wrong(u, v):
    for i in range(LOG - 1, -1, -1):
        if up[i][u] != up[i][v]:
            u, v = up[i][u], up[i][v]
    return up[0][u]
```

The binary-search-style simultaneous lifting in the second phase only produces a correct answer if both nodes start at the **same depth** — the comparison `up[i][u] != up[i][v]` is meaningless if u and v aren't already "aligned" the way linked-list intersection requires equal remaining distance. Skipping the depth-equalization phase and jumping straight to simultaneous lifting silently produces a wrong LCA whenever the two nodes started at different depths, which is the common case, not the exception.
